\label{L:compact}
	The embedding
	$\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{S}\mathcal{O}(2\times2)}\hookrightarrow L^6(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$ is compact.

Lemma \ref{L:equisym} implies that $\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathcal{D}_\mathcal{F}$; moreover, $\mathcal{Y}$ is closed in $\mathcal{D}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$ and infinite dimensional by Lemma \ref{L:inf}. Since $\mathbf{U}\mapsto\frac{\mathbf{U}}{\psi}\circ\pi$ is a linear isometry between $\mathcal{D}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$ and $H^1(\mathbb{S}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$ and between $L^6(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$ and $L^6(\mathbb{S}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$, one easily checks that $E|_{\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{F}}}$ is invariant under the action of $\mathcal{S}\mathcal{O}(2\times2)$. Hence every $\mathbf{U}\in\mathcal{Y}$ is a solution to \eqref{e-crit} if and only if it is a critical point of $E|_\mathcal{Y}$ owing to Theorem \ref{T:Palais}.
	
	We want to make use of \cite[Theorem 9.12]{Rabin}. From the embeddings $\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathcal{D}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)\subset L^6(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$, there exists $\delta>0$ such that
	\[
	\inf\Set{E(\mathbf{U})|\mathbf{U}\in\mathcal{Y}\text{ and }|\nabla\mathbf{U}|_2=\delta}>0
	\]
	(cf. the proof of Lemma \ref{L:I678} \textit{(a)}). In addition, if $Y\subset\mathcal{Y}$ is a finite dimensional subspace, then the norms $|\nabla(\cdot)|_2$ and $|\cdot|_6$ are equivalent in $Y$, thus there exists $R=R(Y)>0$ such that $E(\mathbf{U})\le0$ for every $\mathbf{U}\in Y$ with $|\mathbf{U}|_6\ge R$. We are only left to prove that $E|_\mathcal{Y}$ satisfies the Palais--Smale condition at every positive level, therefore let $\mathbf{U}_n\in\mathcal{Y}$ be a Palais--Smale sequence for $E|_\mathcal{Y}$ at some $c>0$.                                                          
	Similarly to the proof of Theorem \ref{T:Ex} we prove that $\mathbf{U}_n$ is bounded, hence there exists $\mathbf{U}\in\mathcal{Y}$ such that, up to a subsequence,
	\begin{equation}\label{e-wc}
	\mathbf{U}_n\rightharpoonup\mathbf{U} \quad \text{in } \mathcal{D}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)
	\end{equation}
	and, in view of Lemma \ref{L:compact},
	\begin{equation}\label{e-sc}
	\mathbf{U}_n\to\mathbf{U} \quad \text{in } L^6(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3).
	\end{equation}
	Since $\lim_nE|_\mathcal{Y}'(\mathbf{U}_n)=0$, from \eqref{e-wc} and \eqref{e-sc} we obtain
	\begin{equation}\label{e-U}
	0=\lim_nE'(\mathbf{U}_n)(\mathbf{U})=|\nabla\mathbf{U}|_2^2-|\mathbf{U}|_6^6
	\end{equation}
	and, since $\mathbf{U}_n$ is bounded,
	\begin{equation}\label{e-Un}
	0=\lim_nE'(\mathbf{U}_n)(\mathbf{U}_n)=\lim_n|\nabla\mathbf{U}_n|_2^2-|\mathbf{U}|_6^6.
	\end{equation}
	From \eqref{e-U} and \eqref{e-Un} we have $\lim_n|\nabla\mathbf{U}_n|_2=|\nabla\mathbf{U}|_2$, which, together with \eqref{e-wc}, yields $\lim_n\mathbf{U}_n=\mathbf{U}$ in $\mathcal{D}^{1,2}(\mathbb{R}^3,\mathbb{R}^3)$.

From \cite[Theorem 1, Corollary 1]{Clarke} there exist $\tau\ge0$, $(\lambda_j)_{j=1}^m\in[0,\infty[^m$, and $(\sigma_k)_{k=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^n$, not all zero, such that
\begin{equation}\label{e-Clarke1}
\tau f'(\bar{x})+\sum_{j=1}^m \lambda_j\phi_j'(\bar{x})+\sum_{k=1}^n \sigma_k\psi_k'(\bar{x})=0.
\end{equation}
If $\tau>0$, then we can divide both sides of \eqref{e-Clarke1} by $\tau$ and, up to relabelling $\lambda_j$ and $\sigma_k$, conclude the proof, hence assume by contradiction that $\tau=0$, i.e.
\begin{equation}\label{e-Clarke2}
\sum_{j=1}^m \lambda_j\phi_j'(\bar{x})+\sum_{k=1}^n \sigma_k\psi_k'(\bar{x})=0.
\end{equation}
If $\phi_j(\bar{x})<0$ for some $j\in\{1,\dots,m\}$, then of course $\lambda_j=0$, hence, up to considering a (possibly empty) subset of $\{1,\dots,m\}$ in \eqref{e-Clarke2}, we can assume that $\phi_1(\bar{x})=\dots=\phi_{m_0}(\bar{x})=0$ and $\lambda_{m_0+1}=\dots=\lambda_m=0$ for some $0\leq m_0\leq m$, where $m_0=0$ denotes that $\lambda_j=0$ for all $j\in \{1,\dots,m\}$ and $m_0=m$ denotes $\phi_j(\bar{x})=0$ for all $j\in \{1,\dots,m\}$. Then the differential
\[
\bigl(\phi_1'(\bar{x}),\dots,\phi_{m_0}'(\bar{x}),\psi_1'(\bar{x}),\dots,\psi_n'(\bar{x})\bigr)\colon E\to\mathbb{R}^{m_0+n}
\]
is surjective and so, for every $j\in\{1,\dots,m_0\}$ (resp. $k\in\{1,\dots,n\}$), there exists $y\in\mathcal{H}$ such that $\phi_j'(\bar{x})(y)\ne0$, $\phi_i'(\bar{x})(y)=0$ for every $i\in\{1,\dots,m_0\}\setminus\{j\}$ and $\psi_k'(\bar{x})(y)=0$ for every $k\in\{1,\dots,n\}$ (resp. $\psi_k'(\bar{x})(y)\ne0$, $\psi_i'(\bar{x})(y)=0$ for every $i\in\{1,\dots,n\}\setminus\{k\}$ and $\phi_j'(\bar{x})(y)=0$ for every $j\in\{1,\dots,m_0\}$). This and \eqref{e-Clarke2} imply $\lambda_j=0$ for every $j\in\{1,\dots,m_0\}$ and $\sigma_k=0$ for every $k\in\{1,\dots,n\}$, a contradiction.

